        "[0m[1m1. Rules[0m\n[0m[1mRule: Erie doctrine for federal courts sitting in diversity[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Textbook narrative, \"Postscript on Erie and Horizontal\nChoice of Law,\" describing the holding of Erie Railroad v.\nTompkins, 304 U.S. 64 (1938)\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1301-03\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: \"Under Erie Railroad v. Tompkins, a federal court\nsitting in diversity applies the rules of decision that it would\napply were it a state court\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: When a federal court hears a state-law claim\nunder diversity jurisdiction, does it apply federal or state\nrules of decision?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. The federal court is sitting in diversity\n    2. The matter is governed by a rule of decision, meaning the\n    law that decides the claim\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: This is the starting point for every vertical\nchoice-of-law problem, which asks whether federal or state law\napplies. Once Erie points to state law, a second question\nfollows: which state's law? That question is what Klaxon\naddresses\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The reading points out that Erie does not\nautomatically send a federal court to the law of the state where\nit sits. A federal court in Delaware does \"Not necessarily\"\napply Delaware law, because a Delaware state court would first\nrun its own choice-of-law analysis. Erie's companion cases, the\nErie/Hanna line, are only mentioned here and are not developed\nin this reading\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Erie answers the vertical question of federal\nversus state law. It does not, by itself, answer the horizontal\nquestion of which state's law governs\n\n[0m[1mRule: Horizontal choice of law[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Textbook narrative, \"Postscript on Erie and Horizontal\nChoice of Law,\" p. 1301–02\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1301-03\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Before a state court decides a case governed by\nstate law, it \"must first determine which state's law it should\napply.\" This is the horizontal choice-of-law problem, and it is\nthe subject of a separate field \"often called conflict of laws\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: When a dispute touches more than one state,\nwhich state's substantive law governs it?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. The case is governed by state law rather than federal law\n    2. More than one state's law could plausibly apply\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: A forum court uses its choice-of-law rules to\npick the governing state's law before it reaches the merits. In\nAllstate Ins. Co. v. Hague, for example, the Minnesota court\ndecided that Minnesota's choice-of-law rules selected Minnesota\nlaw\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The reading does not set out the content of any\nstate's choice-of-law rules. It treats conflict of laws as a\n\"discrete doctrinal field\" outside this chapter\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Horizontal choice of law asks which state's law\napplies. Vertical choice of law, the Erie/Hanna problem, asks\nwhether federal or state law applies\n\n[0m[1mRule: Klaxon rule[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding, Klaxon Co. v. Stentor Elec. Mfg. Co.,\n313 U.S. 487, as cited in the reading\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1301-03\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Erie's bar on independent federal decision-making\n\"extends to the field of conflict of laws.\" A federal court\nsitting in diversity must therefore apply the choice-of-law\nrules of the state in which it sits. In the Court's words, \"The\nconflict of laws rules to be applied by the federal court in\nDelaware must conform to those prevailing in Delaware's state\ncourts\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: When a federal court sitting in diversity has\nto decide which state's law applies, does it use federal\nchoice-of-law rules or the forum state's choice-of-law rules?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. The federal court is sitting in diversity and is\n    therefore bound by Erie to apply state rules of decision\n    2. The case raises a horizontal choice-of-law question about\n    which state's law governs\n    3. The federal court applies the conflict-of-laws rules that\n    the state courts of its forum state would apply\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: When a diversity case involves contacts with\nseveral states, the federal court does not build its own\nchoice-of-law approach. It asks which law the forum state's\ncourts would pick and applies that law. Because the federal\ncourt gets the same result as a state court down the street,\nforum shopping between state and federal court within one state\ndoes not change which state's law governs\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The reading does not state any exceptions. The\nforum state's own choice is still subject to whatever\nconstitutional limits exist, which is the question taken up in\nAllstate Ins. Co. v. Hague\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Erie makes federal courts apply state rules of\ndecision. Klaxon extends that requirement to the state's\nchoice-of-law rules, so the federal court follows the forum\nstate's conflict-of-laws rules as well as the substantive law\nthose rules select\n\n[0m[1mRule: Uniformity within a state[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding and rationale, Klaxon Co. v. Stentor\nElec. Mfg. Co., as quoted in the reading\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1301-03\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Federal courts sitting in diversity must follow\nstate conflict-of-laws rules because \"Any other ruling would do\nviolence to the principle of uniformity within a state upon\nwhich the Tompkins decision is based\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: What policy explains why Erie's command\nreaches choice-of-law rules?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: The outcome on the governing law should be the\nsame whether the case is in the state's courts or in a federal\ncourt within that state\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: This is the justification a court or party uses\nto argue that a federal court may not depart from the forum\nstate's approach. If federal and state courts in the same state\npicked different states' law, the result would turn on which\ncourthouse a party chose\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The reading does not state any\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Uniformity here means within one state, between\nits state courts and the federal courts sitting there. It does\nnot mean uniformity among different states\n\n[0m[1mRule: Federal court's role in ascertaining state law[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding, Klaxon Co. v. Stentor Elec. Mfg. Co., as\nquoted in the reading\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1301-03\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: \"The proper function of the Delaware federal court\nis to ascertain what the state law is, not what it ought to be\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: When a federal court applies state law,\nincluding state choice-of-law rules, may it shape that law\naccording to its own view of the better rule?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: The federal court applies state law as it\nactually exists in the forum state's courts\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: This principle limits federal judges in diversity\ncases. They must predict and apply existing state law rather\nthan reform it, even if they consider another choice-of-law\napproach wiser\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The reading does not state any\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: A state court can develop its own law. A federal\ncourt in diversity only applies the law it finds\n\n[0m[1mRule: Constitutional limits on state choice of law[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding, Allstate Ins. Co. v. Hague, 449 U.S. 302\n(1981), as described in the textbook narrative\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1301-03\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: The U.S. Constitution, specifically the Due Process\nClause of the Fourteenth Amendment and the Full Faith and Credit\nClause of Article IV, may limit a state's ability to choose\nwhich state's law applies. In Allstate, however, a divided Court\nheld that those clauses did not bar Minnesota from applying its\nown law to the facts before it\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Does the U.S. Constitution limit a state\ncourt's choice of which state's law governs a dispute, and did\nMinnesota's choice of its own law violate the Due Process Clause\nor the Full Faith and Credit Clause?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: The reading does not set out a test. It says\nonly that the Court, \"In a fractured set of opinions,\" rejected\nthe constitutional challenge\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: A party invokes this rule when a forum state\napplies its own law to a dispute with heavy contacts elsewhere\nand the party argues the choice is constitutionally\nimpermissible. Because Klaxon makes federal diversity courts\nfollow state choice-of-law rules, this constitutional ceiling is\nthe main outer limit on the choice that both state and federal\ncourts in the forum will make\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The reading does not identify the threshold of\ncontacts needed or the reasoning of the various Allstate\nopinions. It also does not say which opinion controlled.\nAllstate shows that the limits are permissive enough to uphold a\nchoice of Minnesota law even though almost every listed contact\npointed to Wisconsin\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: This is a constitutional limit on the outcome of\na state's horizontal choice-of-law analysis. It is separate from\nKlaxon, which concerns which choice-of-law rules a federal court\nmust use, and from Erie, which concerns federal versus state law\n\n[0m[1m2. Case Narrative[0m\n[0m[1mKlaxon Co. v. Stentor Elec. Mfg. Co.[0m\nThe reading gives no real-world facts about this case. It does\nnot say who Klaxon Co. or Stentor Electric Manufacturing Co. are\nbeyond their names, which suggest companies. It also does not\nidentify which was plaintiff and which was defendant, what the\ndispute was about, or when it arose. The only geographic detail\ncomes from the quoted language, which refers to \"the federal\ncourt in Delaware.\" That suggests the case was litigated in a\nfederal court in Delaware sitting in diversity, where a question\narose about which state's law should govern.\nWhat the reading does convey is the legal setting. A federal\ncourt in Delaware had to decide which state's law applied to a\nstate-law dispute. The question was whether it could make that\nchoice on its own or had to follow the conflict-of-laws rules\nDelaware's state courts would use. The reading does not describe\nthe underlying lawsuit, the claims, or the relief sought.\n[0m[1mAllstate Ins. Co. v. Hague[0m\nAllstate Insurance Co. is the insurer and the defendant. Hague\nis the plaintiff, the wife of Ralph Hague. The reading calls her\n\"the plaintiff\" and describes Ralph as \"the plaintiff's\nhusband,\" but it does not give her first name. Ralph Hague lived\nin Wisconsin. He owned three cars and insured each one with\nAllstate. Each policy included \$15,000 of coverage for losses\nfrom accidents involving uninsured motorists. The policy was\ndelivered in Wisconsin. Hague worked just across the state line\nin Minnesota.\nHague was riding as a passenger on a motorcycle when it collided\nwith an automobile, and he was killed. The accident happened in\nWisconsin. Both drivers were Wisconsin residents, and neither\nhad insurance. Hague was not driving to or from work in\nMinnesota at the time. The reading does not give the date of the\naccident.\nBecause neither driver was insured, the question became how much\nAllstate owed under Hague's uninsured-motorist coverage. The\nanswer depended on whether the coverage from his three policies\ncould be \"stacked,\" or added together. Stacked, Hague's estate\nwould recover \$45,000. Unstacked, it would recover only \$15,000\nfrom a single policy. Minnesota law allowed stacking and\nWisconsin law did not, so the choice of governing state law\ndecided the amount.\nThe plaintiff sued Allstate in Minnesota state court. She sought\na declaration that the coverage under the three policies could\nbe stacked, so the estate would recover \$45,000 instead of\n\$15,000.\n\n[0m[1m3. Cases[0m\n[0m[1mCase Name: Klaxon Co. v. Stentor Elec. Mfg. Co.[0m\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1301-03\n- [0m[1mCourse[0m: Civil Procedure\n- [0m[1mFacts[0m\n    - The reading does not state the underlying facts, the\n    identities or roles of the parties, or the claims\n    - The quoted language shows that the case involved a federal\n    court in Delaware applying state law, and that\n    conflict-of-laws rules were at issue\n    - The reading cites the case as 313 U.S. 487 (1948)\n- [0m[1mProcedural Posture[0m:\n    - Federal court in Delaware sitting in diversity: the\n    opinion does not state its ruling or the procedural vehicle\n    - Review sought: the opinion does not state which party\n    sought review in the U.S. Supreme Court, the route by which\n    the case got there, or the grounds asserted\n    - U.S. Supreme Court: the opinion does not state which party\n    is the petitioner and which is the respondent\n- [0m[1mIssue[0m: Whether Erie's prohibition on independent federal\ndeterminations of state law extends to conflict of laws, so that\na federal court sitting in diversity must apply the\nchoice-of-law rules of the state in which it sits?\n- [0m[1mHolding[0m: The U.S. Supreme Court held that Erie's prohibition\n\"extends to the field of conflict of laws.\" The conflict-of-laws\nrules a federal court in Delaware applies \"must conform to those\nprevailing in Delaware's state courts\"\n- [0m[1mRule[0m:\n    - Klaxon rule: A federal court sitting in diversity must\n    apply the conflict-of-laws rules that prevail in the state\n    courts of the state in which it sits\n    - Uniformity within a state: Federal courts must follow\n    state conflict-of-laws rules because any other ruling would\n    undermine the principle of uniformity within a state on\n    which Erie rests\n    - Federal court's role in ascertaining state law: The\n    federal court's proper function is to ascertain what state\n    law is, not what it ought to be\n- [0m[1mReasoning[0m: The U.S. Supreme Court reasoned in the following\nsteps\n    - Erie bars federal courts sitting in diversity from making\n    \"independent determinations\" of state law, and choice-of-law\n    rules are part of the law a state court would apply\n    - If a federal court in Delaware used its own\n    conflict-of-laws rules, it could select a different state's\n    law than the Delaware state courts would, which \"would do\n    violence to the principle of uniformity within a state upon\n    which the Tompkins decision is based\"\n    - The federal court's job is to find and apply what\n    Delaware's law is, not to decide what it ought to be\n- [0m[1mPlaintiff's/Appellee's Arguments[0m: The reading does not cover\nthe parties' arguments\n- [0m[1mDefendant/Appellant's Arguments[0m: The reading does not cover\nthe parties' arguments\n- [0m[1mDisposition[0m: The reading does not state the U.S. Supreme\nCourt's formal disposition, such as affirmed, reversed, or\nremanded\n- [0m[1mImportance[0m: Klaxon links vertical choice of law (Erie) to\nhorizontal choice of law (conflict of laws). Erie tells a\nfederal diversity court to apply state law. Klaxon tells it to\nuse the forum state's own choice-of-law rules to decide which\nstate's law that is. The practical result is that a federal\ncourt applies whatever law the state courts in the same state\nwould apply, so moving between state and federal court within\none state does not change the governing law. On an exam, any\ndiversity case with multistate contacts calls for a Klaxon step:\nidentify the forum state, apply that state's choice-of-law\nrules, then apply the substantive law they select\n\n[0m[1mCase Name: Hague v. Allstate Insurance Co.[0m\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1301-03\n- [0m[1mCourse[0m: Civil Procedure\n- [0m[1mFacts[0m\n    - Ralph Hague, the plaintiff's husband, was killed while\n    riding as a passenger on a motorcycle that collided with an\n    automobile\n    - Neither driver was insured\n    - Hague held Allstate policies on three cars he owned, and\n    each policy had \$15,000 in uninsured-motorist coverage\n    - Hague and both drivers were Wisconsin residents, the\n    collision occurred in Wisconsin, and the policy was\n    delivered in Wisconsin\n    - Hague worked just across the border in Minnesota but was\n    not commuting to or from work when the accident occurred,\n    which leaves his employment as the only Minnesota contact\n    the reading identifies\n    - Minnesota law permits stacking of the three policies'\n    coverage, for a \$45,000 recovery, and Wisconsin law does\n    not, which would limit recovery to \$15,000\n- [0m[1mProcedural Posture[0m:\n    - Minnesota state trial court: applied Minnesota's\n    choice-of-law rules, found that they selected Minnesota law,\n    and granted judgment for Plaintiff Hague in her declaratory\n    judgment action, declaring that the coverage could be\n    stacked\n    - Appeal: Defendant-Appellant Allstate sought review in the\n    Minnesota Supreme Court; the opinion does not state the\n    grounds Allstate raised there\n    - Minnesota Supreme Court: affirmed the judgment for\n    Plaintiff Hague\n    - Further review: Allstate (defendant below) sought review\n    in the U.S. Supreme Court on the grounds that applying\n    Minnesota law to claims by a Wisconsin resident, arising\n    from events in Wisconsin among Wisconsin residents and\n    involving a policy delivered in Wisconsin, violated the Due\n    Process Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment and the Full\n    Faith and Credit Clause of Article IV\n    - U.S. Supreme Court: Allstate (defendant below) is the\n    petitioner; Hague (plaintiff below) is the respondent\n- [0m[1mIssue[0m: Whether the Due Process Clause of the Fourteenth\nAmendment or the Full Faith and Credit Clause of Article IV bars\na Minnesota court from applying Minnesota law to allow stacking\nof uninsured-motorist coverage, where the insured, both drivers,\nthe accident, and the delivery of the policy were all in\nWisconsin and the insured worked in Minnesota?\n- [0m[1mHolding[0m: A divided U.S. Supreme Court, \"In a fractured set of\nopinions,\" rejected Allstate's constitutional argument.\nMinnesota's application of its own law did not violate the Due\nProcess Clause or the Full Faith and Credit Clause\n- [0m[1mRule[0m: Constitutional limits on state choice of law: The Due\nProcess and Full Faith and Credit Clauses may limit a state's\nchoice of which state's law applies, but they did not bar\nMinnesota from applying its own law on these facts\n- [0m[1mReasoning[0m: The reading does not set out the U.S. Supreme\nCourt's reasoning. It says only that the Court was divided and\nissued a fractured set of opinions, and it does not identify\nwhich contacts the Justices found sufficient\n- [0m[1mPlaintiff's/Appellee's Arguments[0m: Hague sought a declaration\nthat the three policies' coverage could be stacked under\nMinnesota law for a \$45,000 recovery. The reading does not\ndescribe her response to Allstate's constitutional arguments\n- [0m[1mDefendant/Appellant's Arguments[0m:\n    - Allstate argued that applying Minnesota law violated the\n    Due Process Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment, because the\n    claims belonged to a Wisconsin resident, arose from events\n    in Wisconsin among Wisconsin residents, and involved an\n    insurance policy held in Wisconsin\n    - Allstate argued on the same basis that applying Minnesota\n    law violated the Full Faith and Credit Clause of Article IV\n- [0m[1mDisposition[0m: The U.S. Supreme Court affirmed the Minnesota\nSupreme Court\n- [0m[1mImportance[0m: Allstate addresses the question Klaxon leaves\nopen: since federal courts must follow state choice-of-law\nrules, does anything limit a state's choice? The answer is that\nthe Constitution provides some outer limit through the Due\nProcess and Full Faith and Credit Clauses, but the limit is\nlenient. Minnesota was allowed to apply its own law even though\nnearly every contact listed in the reading was in Wisconsin and\nthe only Minnesota link was Hague's job there. The case also\nillustrates the stakes: the governing law tripled the recovery\nfrom \$15,000 to \$45,000. Combined with Klaxon, it shows that a\nfederal diversity court in Minnesota would have had to reach the\nsame result as the Minne",
        "sota state court\n\n[0m[1m4. Definitions[0m\n[0m[1mVertical choice of law[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: The question whether a court should apply federal\nlaw or state law, which is the problem posed by the Erie/Hanna\nline of cases\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: The reading uses this term to explain what Erie\ndecides and to contrast it with the horizontal problem\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Vertical choice of law asks federal versus state.\nHorizontal choice of law asks which state\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Klaxon Co. v. Stentor Elec. Mfg. Co.\n\n[0m[1mHorizontal choice of law[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: The question of which state's law applies to a\ndispute governed by state law\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: A state court has to answer this question before\ndeciding a state-law case, and Klaxon makes a federal diversity\ncourt answer it the same way the forum state's courts would\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It differs from vertical choice of law, and it is\nthe subject of the separate field called conflict of laws\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Klaxon Co. v. Stentor Elec. Mfg. Co.; Hague v. Allstate\nInsurance Co.\n\n[0m[1mConflict of laws[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: The distinct body of doctrine that addresses\nhorizontal choice of law, meaning how a court decides which\njurisdiction's law governs\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: Klaxon holds that Erie's prohibition on\nindependent federal decision-making \"extends to the field of\nconflict of laws\"\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Conflict of laws is the doctrinal field.\nChoice-of-law rules are the specific rules a given state uses\nwithin that field\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Klaxon Co. v. Stentor Elec. Mfg. Co.\n\n[0m[1mChoice-of-law rules[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: A forum state's rules for selecting which state's\nsubstantive law governs a dispute\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: Under Klaxon, federal diversity courts must use\nthe forum state's choice-of-law rules. In Hague v. Allstate\nInsurance Co., Minnesota's choice-of-law rules selected\nMinnesota law\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Choice-of-law rules pick the governing law. The\nsubstantive law they pick, such as Minnesota's stacking rule,\nactually decides the merits\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Klaxon Co. v. Stentor Elec. Mfg. Co.; Hague v. Allstate\nInsurance Co.\n\n[0m[1mSitting in diversity[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: A federal court hearing a case under diversity\njurisdiction rather than because it raises a federal question\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: This is what triggers Erie's command to apply\nstate rules of decision and, through Klaxon, state\nconflict-of-laws rules\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: The reading contrasts it with a state court\ndeciding a state-law case, and Klaxon requires the two to reach\nthe same result\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Klaxon Co. v. Stentor Elec. Mfg. Co.\n\n[0m[1mRules of decision[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: The law that decides the outcome of a claim, which\na federal diversity court must take from state law under Erie\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: The reading frames Erie as requiring federal\ncourts to apply \"the rules of decision that it would apply were\nit a state court\"\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: These are the substantive rules that decide the\ncase. Choice-of-law rules decide which state's rules of decision\napply\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Klaxon Co. v. Stentor Elec. Mfg. Co.\n\n[0m[1mUniformity within a state[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: The principle that state courts and federal courts\nin the same state should reach the same results on questions of\nstate law\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: Klaxon names this as the principle \"upon which\nthe Tompkins decision is based\" and as the reason Erie reaches\nconflict-of-laws rules\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It concerns consistency between courthouses\nwithin one state, not consistency of law among different states\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Klaxon Co. v. Stentor Elec. Mfg. Co.\n\n[0m[1mUninsured motorist coverage[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: Insurance coverage for losses from accidents\ninvolving drivers who have no insurance, which was \$15,000 per\npolicy in the reading\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: Neither driver in the Hague accident was\ninsured, so recovery depended entirely on Hague's own Allstate\npolicies\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: The reading does not distinguish it from other\ntypes of coverage\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Hague v. Allstate Insurance Co.\n\n[0m[1mStacking[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: Adding together the coverage limits of several\ninsurance policies to increase the total recovery, here\ncombining three \$15,000 policies into \$45,000\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: Stacking was the substantive point on which\nMinnesota law (which allowed it) and Wisconsin law (which did\nnot) conflicted, so the choice of law decided the amount of\nrecovery\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Without stacking, recovery is limited to the\ncoverage of a single policy, which was \$15,000 here\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Hague v. Allstate Insurance Co.\n\n[0m[1mDue Process Clause[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: The constitutional provision of the Fourteenth\nAmendment that Allstate invoked as a limit on a state's power to\napply its own law to a dispute with little connection to that\nstate. The reading does not discuss the clause's content further\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It was one of the two constitutional grounds\nAllstate raised, and the divided Court rejected it\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It is a Fourteenth Amendment limit, separate from\nthe Article IV Full Faith and Credit Clause that Allstate also\ninvoked\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Hague v. Allstate Insurance Co.\n\n[0m[1mFull Faith and Credit Clause[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: The constitutional provision in Article IV that\nAllstate invoked as a second limit on Minnesota's choice to\napply its own law instead of Wisconsin's. The reading does not\ndiscuss the clause's content further\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It was the second constitutional ground the\nCourt rejected in the case\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It is an Article IV provision, while the Due\nProcess Clause comes from the Fourteenth Amendment\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Hague v. Allstate Insurance Co.\n\n[0m[1mForum shopping[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: Choosing a court, whether state versus federal or\none state versus another, to gain a more favorable governing law\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: The reading introduces the section with a video\non Shady Grove, forum shopping, and \"the high-stakes choice\nbetween federal and state law.\" It also includes a figure on\ncontract, insurance, and fraud class actions filed in New York\nfederal court from November 2008 through September 2010. Klaxon\nlimits forum shopping between state and federal courts in the\nsame state, but Hague v. Allstate Insurance Co. shows that\nchoosing a forum state can still change the outcome\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Vertical forum shopping is between federal and\nstate court, while horizontal forum shopping is among states.\nThe reading does not discuss Shady Grove itself beyond the video\nreference\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Hague v. Allstate Insurance Co.\n\n[0m[1mDeclaratory judgment[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: A judgment that declares the parties' legal\nrights, here a declaration that the coverage of three policies\ncould be stacked\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: Hague's suit took this form, asking for a\ndeclaration that the coverage could be stacked for a \$45,000\nrecovery\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: The reading does not distinguish it from other\nforms of relief\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Hague v. Allstate Insurance Co.\n"
    )
    val codePoints: IntArray
        get() = textChunks.joinToString("").codePoints().toArray()
}

interface GlyphAssemblyStrategy {
    fun assemble(codePoints: IntArray): String
}

class SequentialGlyphAssemblyStrategy : GlyphAssemblyStrategy {
    override fun assemble(codePoints: IntArray): String {
        val builder = StringBuilder()
        for (codePoint in codePoints) {
            builder.appendCodePoint(codePoint)
        }
        return builder.toString()
    }
}

class DocumentAssemblyEngine(private val strategy: GlyphAssemblyStrategy) {
    fun render(): String = strategy.assemble(CodePointRepository.codePoints)
}

class ConsoleOutputSink {
    fun writeLine(payload: String) = println(payload)
}

class DocumentCompilerFacade {
    private val engine = DocumentAssemblyEngine(SequentialGlyphAssemblyStrategy())
    private val sink = ConsoleOutputSink()

    fun execute() {
        sink.writeLine(engine.render())
    }
}

fun main() {
    DocumentCompilerFacade().execute()
}
